 <h2 id="phal"><code>phal</code></h2>
-<p class="shesh-desc">layer: core A Result is a 2-cell mala supplied by the caller: [ok, value]. ok = 1 means success and value holds the answer; ok = 0 means failure and value is 0. The checked functions can produce Results directly (result_checked_*), and phal_se_siddhi bridges the older ok out-parameter convention into a Result. What this buys over the ok convention (docs/ERROR-CONVENTION.md): a single value carries both the flag and the answer, so a Result can be stored, passed, and returned from a function without threading a separate ok cell through every call, and phal_mulya_va handles both branches in one expression. What it still cannot do without compiler support: nothing forces the caller to check phal_asti_siddhi (the value can still be read unchecked, exactly like ignoring ok); there is no propagation operator (the caller still branches by hand); and there is no chaining/map/and_then because the language has no callbacks. A Result is a caller-owned mutable array — aliasing and mutation hazards remain (no ownership model). The hand-written substitute for propagation is the early-return chain: after each producing step, `yadi (phal_asti_siddhi(t) == 0)` writes the failure into out and returns at once, so the failure path is one branch per step instead of nested conditionals. See docs/ERROR-CONVENTION.md and tests/stdlib/result_chain.sm.</p>
