 <h2 id="roop"><code>roop</code></h2>
-<p class="shesh-desc">layer: alloc Number and string formatting on BYTE strings (packed bytes, explicit lengths, `strb` idiom): every function writes packed bytes into `out` with a 0 terminator and returns the byte count. This is the modern idiom; the older `lib/strconv.smlib` works on CELL arrays (mala of char codes) and has no width/alignment/grouping/fixed-decimal/util_chinha surface — fmt is the formatting module, strconv is the conversion module. Do not mix the representations: fmt outputs are bytes (read with char_at), strconv outputs are cells (read with [i]). Defaults, stated once here and at each function: - Bases: no prefix unless the _pre / flags form is used. - Grouping: roop_varg defaults to INDIAN grouping (12,34,567). - Rounding: roop_sthir rounds HALF AWAY FROM ZERO (2.5 -&gt; 3, -2.5 -&gt; -3). - Centre padding: the extra pad byte goes RIGHT. - INT64_MIN (-2^63) is handled: |MIN| = 2^63 does not fit in int64, so the digit writers split it (hi/lo) instead of negating it. Every int64 value works. - roop_sthir is exact while |x| * 10^places &lt; 2^52; beyond that the +0.5 rounding step is below float precision. |x| * 10^places must fit in int64. places is clamped to 0..9. All signatures stay within the 6-argument cap. --- integer digits (private helper) --- roop_ank(v, out) — write the decimal digits of |v| (no util_chinha) into out as packed bytes. Returns the digit count. Internal; the public formatters below build on it.</p>
