-<p class="shesh-desc">layer: alloc Elements are QWORD CELLS (the mala/kosh convention), not byte strings. Users with byte strings store POINTERS (suchi_khip(l, ptr)); the caller keeps the pointed-to buffers alive. No mixing: every element here is a qword. dasham elements are NOT supported — an untyped parameter truncates a dasham argument to its integer part. Two handle layouts (do NOT mix families on one handle): - list / stack handle: header [len, cap, data_ptr]; data is a separate cell array. The HANDLE address is stable across growth — only the data pointer inside it moves. - queue handle: header [len, cap, data_ptr, head]; a RING BUFFER (see avali_pravesh). queue_* on a list handle (or list_* on a queue handle) reads the wrong header and corrupts memory. Growth rule: when len == cap, capacity DOUBLES, a new data array is allocated, elements are copied in logical order, and the header&#x27;s data pointer is updated. The old data array is NOT freed — the language has no deallocation primitive, so growth leaks the previous array. Capacity never shrinks. Allocation failure: if nirmmita returns 0 during growth, the operation returns 0 (or ok[0] = 0) and the structure is UNCHANGED. suchi_srij returns 0 if either allocation fails. Out-of-bounds reads: suchi_grah returns 0 for i outside [0, len). 0 is a legal stored value, so check suchi_maap first when it matters. suchi_sangrah / suchi_nivesh / suchi_apanay return 1 ok / 0 out of bounds (or allocation failure for insert). chay_apanay / chay_avalok / avali_nishkas / avali_avalok return the value and set ok[0] (a 1-cell mala) to 1 on success, 0 when empty — the value alone cannot signal emptiness. All signatures stay within the 6-argument cap.</p>
